 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfGc4JQKE1E/",
 "title": "Explain Your Career When You Do Multiple Things",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "When asked what you do, listing every single task causes people to stop listening. Instead, use the Common Thread Technique to lead with the main problem you solve and mention your specific activities as subsets.",
 "key_points": [
  "Most people list multiple jobs like bookkeeping, writing newsletters, and podcasting, causing listeners to tune out by the third item.",
  "Use the Common Thread Technique to explain your career.",
  "Lead with what all your work has in common, which is usually the problem you solve for people.",
  "Then talk about the specific things you do as a subset of that main problem.",
  "Example script: \"I help small business owners understand their finances so they can make better decisions. Right now, I do that through my bookkeeping business, and as part of that, I write a weekly newsletter and I have a podcast.\"",
  "This technique covers all your work without leaving anything out and shows clarity."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Dorie Clark. Here’s the 9-second trick to explaining your career when you do more than one thing without sounding scattered. Someone asks, \"So what do you do?\" And the honest answer is, a lot of things. It's a hard question. So do you go through everything, or pick one and then leave out most of your work? I wrote a book about building a career with multiple income streams called Entrepreneurial You, and here is what to say. Most people try to fit it all in. \"I do bookkeeping, I write a newsletter, and I'm starting a podcast, etc.\" But by the third thing, they've stopped listening. Instead, use what I call the Common Thread Technique. Here is how it works. You lead with what all your work has in common, which is usually a problem you solve for people. Then you talk about the things you do as a subset of that, and it makes a lot more sense to people. You can say, \"I help small business owners understand their finances so they can make better decisions. Right now, I do that through my bookkeeping business. And as part of that, I write a weekly newsletter and I have a podcast.\" That's it. It's about 9 seconds. It works because the common thread covers all your work, so you're not leaving anything out, and the example makes it concrete. It also shows you're clear about what you offer, not all over the place. So if you do a lot of things, try the Common Thread Technique to explain them, and send this to your favorite Renaissance person. Text on screen: How to explain your career when you do more than one thing without sounding scattered. So what do you do?",
